There are a variety of techniques which could be used to transduce a changing magnetic field into an electrical current. This could be potentially very useful for creating a magnetic encoder which is electronically read out.

The first technique to be discussed is anisotropic magnetoresistance (AMR). This phenomena creates a different electrical resistance depending on the relative direction of the magnetization as compared to the current. In this case, our AMR material would be laid out in a strip underneath the device and would become magnetized by the device moving overhead. 
Note that in this application we must be careful to distinguish between magnetic coercivity (a permanent "plastic" change in the magnetic field) and magnetic permeability (a temporary "elastic" change). We would want a material with a very high permeability and ideally a low coercivity as well. High permeability means that the field is easily deflected and low coercivity means that the field is not easily changed permanently. 
Unfortunately, we can't necessarily control the magnetization axis relative to the current direction which leads to random sensor performance. Another potential issue is the material compatibility of the AMR material (likely permalloy or Fe-Ni) with the buffer solution, although this could potentially be addressed by a capping oxide layer. The smallest AMR sensor I could find was on a 10um scale which is much too large for us. https://www.spiedigitallibrary.org/conference-proceedings-of-spie/14081/1408106/AMR-sensors-and-laser-micromachined-permanent-micromagnets-for-microscale-device/10.1117/12.3104570.short

The next techique I looked in to was giant magnetoresistance. These are potentially nanoscale compatible but are difficult to assemble due to the large numbers of precise material layers required. https://pubs.acs.org/ancac3/article-abstract/20/8/6644/5151735/Low-Noise-Nanoscale-Vortex-Sensor-for-Out-of-Plane?redirectedFrom=fulltext I was unable to download the full manuscript but here's an example. These approaches are very powerful but I can't find any examples of nanoscale patterning. This paper was quite interesting to show that the materials for detecting GMR must be made to extreme precision to perform better than TMR sensors. https://sci-hub.st/10.1063/1.109387

Similarly, tunneling magnetoresistance (TMR) and magnetic tunnel junction (MTJ) sensors establish a specific field configuration by stacking materials separated by non-magnetic layers (typically copper). This 2025 thesis was very helpful to review all magnetic sensing technologies and also specifies a workflow to make nanoscale magnetic TMR sensors. Unfortunately it is very complicated. https://kilthub.cmu.edu/articles/thesis/Nano_Magnetic_tunneling_Junction_MTJ_sensor_for_on-chip_current_sensing/28920656?file=54148244

Another potential candidate are SQUIDs (Superconducting Quantum Interference Device), commonly used in quantum computing. These are also much too large and difficult to make, for example no smaller than 300nm if individually made by FIB: https://journals.aps.org/prapplied/abstract/10.1103/3qfq-4458

Overall, direct magnetic field sensing is quite challenging. For example, although magnetic hard drive domains are a similar size (and therefore sensitivity) to what we want to detect, only one HDD reader head is necessary, and therefore it is frequency micron-sized with a nanoscale tip. This is inherently unsuitable for our application.

One class of techniques is based on the Hall effect, which is that moving electrons (current) is deflected in a magnetic field. Anomalous Hall effect is basically the same thing but the magnetic field comes from the material itself (i.e. ferromagnetically induced). There are nanoscale hall effect sensors which can be made out of graphene, GaAs, or other materials. https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-019-50823-8 Table 1 is especially helpful. 
However, this device has a frequency limit of about 100Hz for applied fields of 1 Tesla. This would be potentially attractive but this performance was shown in a cryostat under vacuum, which is very different than our intended use.

Moreover, ferrite (Fe3O4) nanoparticles are superparamagnetic, meaning that they are only magnetized when a field is applied to them.

Overall I think magnetic encoders may be too difficult.
